			<outline text="Selective Availability">
				<outline text="rjolivier@mac.com &lt;mailto:rjolivier@mac.com&gt;

to adam
	 More options	  Aug 26 (3 days ago)
Robert in Los Angeles [1] sent this email to you through the Radio UserLand [2] community server, re this page [3].


Adam,


I work with GPS on cell phones all day and I can tell you that your listener that said commercial GPS is less accurate that military GPS due to induced errors is BS.  It was called Selective Availability and it was turned off in May, 2000.  A commercial GPS with a good stable clock and a good antenna design is accurate to better than 5 meters.


Military GPS is better becasue the GPS satellites actually transmit data on two frequencies, only one of which is available to commecial GPS receivers.  Military units listen on both frequencies. The reason for this is that the a large source of error is caused by ionospheric interferences in the atmosphere.  The ionosphere causes slight delay in the signal reaching earth but this delay is inversely proportional to the frequency of the signal.  Hence, by using two frequencies, a  military receiver can detect the ionospheric delay difference between the two frequencies and cancel it leading to mugh higher accuracy.


Additionally, bombs are moving entities.  The last source of GPS error is called multipath reflection where the GPS signal bounces off of objects and causes the receiver to see duplicates of the signal delayed by various amounts of time.  This happens mostly in urban environments where the signal bounces off of tall buildings.  A moving GPS receiver is able to see the difference in the multipath characteristics as it is moving through the terrain and create a model that cancels out most of them where a stationary unit cannot.  Since a bomb is definitely moving, it is able to cancel the multipath out with great accuracy.


These two factors are soley responsible for why a smart bomb is so accurate, not induced errors.


Check the wikipedia ... en.wikipedia.org—Selective_availability &lt;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selective_availability&gt;



Peace out.


robert"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="&quot;PODCAST FOR HURRICANE VICTIMS&quot;">
			<outline text="wichitarutherford@mac.com &lt;mailto:wichitarutherford@mac.com&gt;

to WichitaRutherf.
	 More options	  12:54 am (12 hours ago)
Hi everybody.  Wichita Rutherford here.  Why don't we do this as the
podcasting community.  Let's all do 2 to 3 minute podcasts that will
inspire people to give to the Red Cross or food banks or whatever
organization they choose to help these folks who are gonna be without
food, water, shelter, clothing, medicine and Lord knows what all
else.  This thing could get ugly quick.  So if we went ahead and
prepared, we'd be ready to do what we do best to help them.  We have
a big infrastructure now with millions of listeners, let's put
ourselves and our podcasts to the side for a minute and put links to
these organizations (whichever ones you choose) as the main focus and
links on our sites.   I mean on the front page too.  There are lots
of us now and I think we've got enough ears listening that might help
make a difference to these people.  Let's keep it simple and call it
&quot;PODCASTING FOR HURRICANE VICTIMS&quot;.   Let's not have a site to go to
or anything for it, let's just put that phrase on our sites, and send
listeners on to where they can help.  In a nutshell it would look
like this:




 I'M PODCASTING FOR HURRICANE VICTIMS.
Please help by donating your time/money/assistance to one of the
following organizations:


LINK - LINK - LINK


Even a little will help a lot.  Thank you so much _______.




Then do a short 1 to 2 minute podcast asking folks to help the same
way.  No joking around, no bad language.  Just as serious as you can
get with your delivery.  Thank them, then sign off.


Or send a copy of it to me if you want and I'll put it on the
&quot;PODCAST FOR HURRICANE VICTIMS&quot; Podcast.   Or do both.  The first one
goes up tonight.  (Sunday)


Let's do this as the podcasting community.  Let's show how powerful
this medium actually is.  This goes for the directories too.
PodcastAlley.com
PodcastDirectory.com  PodcastPickle.com  ALL of you in charge of
podcast directories.  Pass this on to your podcast host connections,
send it to everyone you know who does a podcast.  Professional or
beginner.   iTunes and Disney as well as your cousin in Toledo who's
only done 2 shows.  Don't even email me back, just get started
forwarding this message.  Let's get ready to help these people.
They're gonna need it.  Let's PODCAST FOR HURRICANE VICTIMS.




Podcasting for hurricane victims, your pal,


Wichita


5MinutesWithWichita.com"/>
